    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING',
      content:
          '**Hormone Therapy Management During Rehab:**\n\n**DVT Risk with Estrogen in SCI (Critical Issue):**\n- SCI itself carries a **markedly elevated DVT risk** — up to 80% in untreated patients in the first 3 months. Standard DVT prophylaxis (LMWH or UFH) is mandatory.\n- Exogenous estrogen further increases DVT risk by 2-4x through increased clotting factor production and decreased protein S.\n- **Combined risk:** The synergistic DVT risk of SCI + estrogen therapy requires careful risk-benefit analysis.\n- **Options:**\n  1. **Continue estrogen with enhanced prophylaxis:** Transition from subcutaneous heparin to therapeutic-dose enoxaparin (1 mg/kg BID) for the acute high-risk period (first 3 months). Add compression stockings and sequential compression devices. Monitor with serial D-dimer and lower extremity duplex ultrasound.\n  2. **Temporarily reduce estrogen dose:** Lower estradiol dose during the acute DVT risk period and resume full dose after 3 months when baseline SCI DVT risk decreases.\n  3. **Temporarily discontinue estrogen (NOT recommended as first option):** Abruptly stopping hormone therapy causes significant psychological distress, return of dysphoria, and physical symptoms (hot flashes, mood instability). Only consider if DVT occurs despite enhanced prophylaxis.\n- **Recommended approach:** Continue estrogen at current dose with enhanced DVT prophylaxis. The psychological harm of stopping hormones in a vulnerable patient is significant and the risk can be managed with aggressive anticoagulation.\n- **Endocrinology consultation** for collaborative decision-making.\n\n**Spironolactone Considerations:**\n- Anti-androgen + potassium-sparing diuretic\n- Monitor potassium closely — SCI patients may have altered renal function\n- May contribute to orthostatic hypotension in combination with SCI autonomic dysfunction\n- Continue unless potassium elevated or symptomatic hypotension\n\n**Body Habitus Considerations:**\n- 4 years of estrogen therapy has reduced upper body muscle mass — functional expectations for transfers and wheelchair propulsion may differ from cisgender male norms at T6 level\n- Breast development affects prone positioning for pressure relief and may require modified transfer techniques\n- Wheelchair seating must accommodate female fat distribution pattern',
