      content:
          '**Q: The patient asks about sexual function after SCI, specifically as a transgender woman. How do you approach this conversation?**\n\n**A:** Sexual health counseling for transgender patients with SCI requires specialized knowledge:\n\n1. **Initiate the conversation proactively:** Do not wait for the patient to ask. "Sexual health is an important part of rehabilitation that we discuss with all our SCI patients. I want to make sure we address this in a way that is relevant and respectful to you. May I ask some questions about your sexual health concerns?"\n\n2. **Assessment of function:** At T6 ASIA A, she retains reflex erection capability (sacral reflex arc intact — bulbocavernosus reflex present). Psychogenic arousal pathways (T11-L2) are disrupted. Sensation will be absent in the genital area.\n\n3. **Transgender-specific considerations:**\n- Estrogen therapy has likely altered erectile function and ejaculatory capacity pre-injury — many trans women on estrogen experience reduced erections and libido, which may align with or conflict with her sexual preferences\n- Her sexual practices and what constitutes satisfying sexual activity should be explored without assumptions — she may or may not use her natal genitalia during sexual activity\n- If she is considering future gender-affirming genital surgery (vaginoplasty), SCI adds surgical complexity — sensation, blood supply, and positioning considerations. This is a discussion for later in recovery but should be mentioned\n\n4. **Counseling approach:**\n- Address the patient AND her partner (with permission) — partner sexual adjustment is equally important\n- Discuss alternative erogenous zones above the injury level\n- Referral to a sex therapist experienced with both SCI and transgender patients (may require telemedicine for specialized expertise)\n- Address fertility preservation if relevant — sperm cryopreservation counseling if she has not already addressed this\n\n5. **Resources:** Refer to SCI-specific sexual health resources that are LGBTQ+ inclusive. Many traditional SCI sexual health materials are cisnormative and may not apply.',
