      content:
          '**Rooming and Privacy Accommodations:**\n- **Room assignment:** She should be roomed as a female patient, consistent with her gender identity and legal documentation. If the facility has semi-private rooms, she should be roomed with a female patient.\n- **If no private rooms are available and a female roommate objects:** She has the legal and ethical right to appropriate rooming. The objecting patient should be offered a room change — the transgender patient should NOT be moved to accommodate the objection.\n- **Privacy for personal care:** Ensure privacy during catheterization, bowel care, bathing, and dressing. Use of a consistent care team who she is comfortable with is ideal.\n\n**Catheterization Considerations:**\n- She has male urethral anatomy — intermittent catheterization technique uses male catheter length (16 French, 40 cm)\n- Teach self-catheterization using correct anatomical approach while using affirming language\n- If she is uncomfortable with self-catheterization due to genital dysphoria, acknowledge this: "I understand that this part of your care may be particularly uncomfortable given how you feel about your body. We can work together to find an approach that minimizes distress."\n- Consider options: self-catheterization with mirror avoidance technique, touchless/closed system catheter kits, or if severe dysphoria, discuss suprapubic catheter as an alternative (though this has its own complications)\n\n**Appropriate Physical Examination:**\n- Perform examinations relevant to the clinical situation — genital/rectal exam for SCI is medically necessary\n- Explain the medical necessity clearly before any genital examination\n- Offer choice of examiner gender when possible\n- Use affirming anatomical language or the patient\'s preferred terms\n- Breast examination as clinically indicated (breast tissue is present from hormone therapy and has the same cancer screening needs as cisgender women)\n\n**Rehabilitation Program:**\n- Standard T6 ASIA A rehabilitation protocol: wheelchair mobility training, transfers, pressure relief, bowel and bladder management, upper extremity strengthening, community reintegration\n- Account for reduced upper body muscle mass from estrogen therapy in functional goal-setting — she may need a lighter wheelchair, may take longer to achieve independent transfers than cisgender male T6 SCI norms\n- Orthostatic hypotension management — spironolactone may exacerbate; monitor closely\n- Autonomic dysreflexia education — T6 level is at the threshold; catheterization and bowel care are common triggers\n\n**Emotional Support:**\n- The intersection of new SCI with pre-existing gender identity challenges creates a compounded adjustment burden\n- Psychology referral: therapist experienced with both SCI adjustment and gender identity\n- Peer support: connect with LGBTQ+ SCI support networks if available\n- Monitor for recurrence of depression/anxiety',
