      content:
          '**Staff Education on Gender-Affirming Care:**\n- **Pre-admission huddle:** Before the patient arrives, brief the nursing staff, therapy team, and support staff on:\n  - Correct name and pronouns (she/her) — document prominently in the chart\n  - The patient is a woman — treat and address her as such in all interactions\n  - Gender identity is not a diagnosis, a curiosity, or a discussion topic among staff\n  - Questions about surgical status or genitalia should be limited to what is clinically necessary\n- **Ongoing education:** Arrange in-service training on LGBTQ+ culturally competent care. The Fenway Institute and WPATH provide healthcare provider education resources.\n- **Zero tolerance for misgendering:** Intentional or repeated misgendering (using wrong pronouns or former name) is a form of harassment and should be addressed through the same channels as any other unprofessional behavior.\n\n**Documentation — Legal Name vs Chosen Name:**\n- **EHR documentation:** Use her legal name (which matches her female identity and legal documents) as the primary name in the medical record. This is consistent with her insurance and legal identification.\n- **If legal name change has NOT occurred (different scenario):** Many EHR systems now support a "chosen name" or "preferred name" field. The chosen name should appear on wristbands, door cards, and communication. The legal name remains in the demographic/billing section.\n- **Pronoun documentation:** Document pronouns (she/her) in a visible location in the chart — many EHRs have a dedicated field.\n- **Problem list:** "Gender dysphoria" should only appear on the problem list if clinically active and relevant to current care. Being transgender is not a diagnosis.\n- **Organ inventory:** For clinical safety, document the patient\'s anatomy accurately (e.g., "Patient has male urogenital anatomy — intermittent catheterization per male technique"). This is medically necessary documentation, not misgendering.\n\n**Regulatory Framework:**\n- Section 1557 of the ACA prohibits discrimination based on gender identity in healthcare settings receiving federal funding\n- Joint Commission standards include respectful, non-discriminatory care regardless of gender identity\n- Many states have additional protections\n- Facility should have a written gender identity non-discrimination policy',
