    Section(
      title: '+ Systems question',
      content:
          '**Q: A CNA on the rehab unit tells you privately, "I have religious beliefs that prevent me from using preferred pronouns. I will provide good physical care, but I cannot call a biological male \'she.\'" How do you handle this?**\n\n**A:** This requires firm boundary-setting while maintaining professionalism:\n\n1. **Acknowledge the CNA\'s honesty** in raising this privately rather than misgendering the patient publicly. This is an opportunity for education, not punitive action (initially).\n\n2. **Set a clear boundary:** "I appreciate that you shared this with me directly. I respect that you have personal beliefs, and those beliefs are your right. However, in this clinical setting, addressing patients by their correct name and pronouns is a professional requirement, not an optional preference. Using the correct pronouns is part of providing respectful care, the same way you would address any patient by their correct name.\n\nThis patient\'s legal name is her female name and her legal gender marker is female. Using \'she/her\' pronouns is both medically and legally accurate."\n\n3. **Frame it as patient safety:** "Misgendering a patient causes measurable psychological harm. In a patient who already has anxiety and depression history, and who is adjusting to a life-changing injury, being misgendered by her care team could trigger a mental health crisis, reduce her engagement in rehabilitation, and negatively impact her outcomes. As healthcare providers, our obligation is to \'first, do no harm.\' This includes psychological harm."\n\n4. **Offer accommodations within limits:** "If you feel you cannot use the correct pronouns despite this guidance, I will need to reassign you from this patient\'s care. That is not a punishment — it is ensuring the patient receives appropriate care and you are not placed in a situation that conflicts with your conscience. However, I want to be clear: the expectation for all staff is to use correct names and pronouns for all patients. This may come up again with future patients."\n\n5. **Escalation if needed:** If the CNA refuses to use correct pronouns AND refuses reassignment, this becomes a human resources matter. Document the conversation and involve nursing leadership.\n\n6. **Systemic solution:** Advocate for facility-wide mandatory LGBTQ+ competency training as part of annual required education (alongside cultural competency, safety training, etc.).',
