    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN E: INTERPERSONAL AND COMMUNICATION SKILLS',
      content:
          '**Scenario:** The patient\'s assigned roommate (a 55-year-old woman recovering from a stroke) requests a room change, telling the nurse, "I am not comfortable sharing a room with that person. I can tell she is really a man. I don\'t feel safe changing clothes in front of a man."\n\n**Mediating While Protecting Patient Dignity:**\n\n**Step 1 — Talk to the roommate privately (without the transgender patient present):**\n\n"I understand you have a concern about your roommate. I want to address this respectfully. Your roommate is a woman. She is a transgender woman, which means she was assigned male at birth but is legally and medically a woman. She has been living as a woman for years, is on hormone therapy, and her legal documents reflect her female gender.\n\nI understand this may be unfamiliar to you, and I am not here to judge your feelings. But I want to be clear: she has the legal and medical right to be housed in a room consistent with her gender identity, and our facility has a non-discrimination policy that protects her."\n\n**Step 2 — Address the safety concern:**\n"You said you do not feel safe. Can you help me understand what specifically concerns you? If it is about physical privacy during changing or personal care, I can assure you that all patients have access to privacy curtains, and personal care for both of you is always performed in private. Your roommate is recovering from a spinal cord injury and requires significant assistance — she is not a threat to your safety in any way."\n\n**Step 3 — Offer a constructive resolution:**\n"If you remain uncomfortable, I can explore whether a room change is available — but I want to be transparent that if a room change happens, it will be YOU who moves, not her. She has done nothing wrong and will not be displaced because of another patient\'s discomfort with her identity.\n\nHowever, I would encourage you to give it some time. Many people who are initially uncomfortable find that once they get to know their roommate as a person, the discomfort fades. She is a talented graphic designer with a supportive partner, and she is facing one of the hardest challenges of her life right now — just like you."\n\n**Step 4 — Protect the transgender patient:**\n- Do NOT inform the transgender patient about the roommate\'s complaint in detail — this would cause harm and violate the spirit of creating a safe environment\n- If the roommate continues to make hostile or derogatory comments, she should be moved immediately\n- If the transgender patient senses hostility and asks to be moved, accommodate her request — but frame it as her choice, not as a response to the roommate\'s prejudice\n- Document the incident for risk management and as data supporting the need for facility-wide education\n\n**Step 5 — Use this as a teaching moment for staff:**\n- Brief the nursing team on how to handle similar situations\n- Reinforce that the transgender patient\'s room assignment is correct and should never be changed to accommodate another patient\'s bias\n- Provide scripts for staff: "All patients are assigned rooms based on their gender identity, in accordance with hospital policy and federal law"',
