      content:
          '**Q: During a team conference, a therapist refers to the patient as "the transgender patient in Room 6" rather than by name. Another therapist asks, "So is it a he or a she?" How do you address this in the moment?**\n\n**A:** Intervene immediately and calmly — this is a teachable moment, not a disciplinary one:\n\n"Let me pause here for a moment. Her name is [patient\'s name], and she uses she/her pronouns. Let us refer to her by name, the way we do for every other patient in our conference.\n\nTo answer the question directly — she is a woman. She is a transgender woman, which means \'she\' is the correct pronoun. If you are ever unsure about a patient\'s pronouns, it is completely appropriate to check the chart where it is documented, or to ask the patient respectfully.\n\nI also want to flag something else — referring to her as \'the transgender patient\' rather than by name defines her by one aspect of her identity rather than as a whole person. We do not say \'the diabetic patient in Room 8\' — we say their name. Let us extend the same courtesy.\n\nI know these conversations can feel uncomfortable, and I appreciate that you all are open to learning. No one is expected to be perfect, but we are expected to try. If you make a pronoun mistake, correct yourself briefly and move on — do not over-apologize, as that makes it about you rather than the patient.\n\nNow, let us get back to her rehabilitation plan. She is making excellent progress with transfers and is ahead of schedule on wheelchair mobility..."\n\nThis response:\n- Corrects the behavior without shaming\n- Provides clear guidance on correct language\n- Normalizes asking when uncertain\n- Models the behavior you expect\n- Redirects to clinical care to avoid making the patient\'s identity the focus of the conference\n- Sets a standard that applies to all future patients',
